# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# Local npm cache directory
#
# npm creates `.npm/` in the project root for its cache when HOME is unusual
# (notably when running as root under systemd or over SSH, where HOME may be
# unset or point somewhere unwritable). It is a cache, never source.
#
# Left untracked, it made `git status` dirty and deploy/update.sh refused to
# run — permanently. That guard exists to protect hand edits from the
# `git reset --hard` in deploy.sh; a regenerable cache is not that. Ignoring it
# here fixes the deploy at the source rather than teaching the guard to
# tolerate arbitrary untracked directories.
.npm/

# env files
#
# `.env*` alone also matches `.env.example`, which is meant to be committed --
# it is documentation, not a secret. deploy/.env.example was silently untracked
# because of this, so the file existed on disk and never reached the server.
# Un-ignore the examples explicitly.
.env*
!.env.example
!*.env.example

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# Local database (SQLite data + WAL sidecar files).
#
# /data/ is ignored wholesale rather than by extension. The earlier narrow
# patterns (*.db, *.db-shm, *.db-wal) matched only that exact suffix, so
# timestamped backups such as trip-packer.db.bak-20260922-095154 slipped
# through and were committed -- carrying real trip data. Anything in here is
# machine-local state and must never be pushed.
/data/
